-0DPPuhL8","type":"song"},{"id":143449891252406,"artist_id":194771532019839,"artist":"Bill Haley","title":"Crazy Man Crazy","year":null,"kind":"s","ess":0,"yt":"MI2vUzUqeZY","type":"song"},{"id":212863851148373,"artist_id":271305225713583,"artist":"Roscoe Gordon","title":"No More Doggin'","year":null,"kind":"s","ess":0,"yt":"Is62Y7_U1-Y","type":"song"},{"id":9847353165646,"artist_id":182833516532380,"artist":"Elvis Presley","title":"Heartbreak Hotel","year":null,"kind":"s","ess":0,"yt":"e9BLw4W5KU8","type":"song"},{"id":242403379186170,"artist_id":110948154991595,"artist":"Buddy Holly","title":"Everyday","year":null,"kind":"s","ess":0,"yt":"GMezwtB1oCU","type":"song"},{"id":198369809873988,"artist_id":110948154991595,"artist":"Buddy Holly","title":"It's So Easy","year":null,"kind":"s","ess":0,"yt":"b53eAQziszc","type":"song"},{"id":277658650364539,"artist_id":110948154991595,"artist":"Buddy Holly","title":"Well All Right","year":null,"kind":"s","ess":0,"yt":"P5AnUctpQXI","type":"song"},{"id":234665701236242,"artist_id":175186537849538,"artist":"Pete Seeger","title":"Bantu Choral Folk Songs","year":null,"kind":"a","ess":0,"yt":"QPTh48wgi5k","type":"album"},{"id":29281065886458,"artist_id":131268107350740,"artist":"Martin Denny","title":"Exotica","year":null,"kind":"a","ess":0,"yt":"Da37N6DsXvI","type":"album"},{"id":152040431025022,"artist_id":246130652362395,"artist":"Ray Charles","title":"I Got A Woman","year":null,"kind":"s","ess":0,"yt":"CnI_LuCJ4Ek","type":"song"}],"labels":[],"link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#rocknroll"}],"similar_scenes":[],"aka":[],"rym":[],"artists":[{"id":109619516280077,"type":"artist","name":"Wesley Willis","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":54,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"works":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Birth of Rock'n'Roll (1950s)","body":"Rock'n'Roll emerged in the 1950s from a confluence of musical developments, including the evolution of rhythm and blues from jazz, and its fusion with country, western, hillbilly, and traditional blues music. This new genre was a direct descendant of the blues, its genesis ultimately linked to the Great Migration and the abolition of slavery. \"Add in country, western, hillbilly, and traditional blues, and you have the conditions for the birth of rock in the 1950s.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Big Picture Stuff: Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Pi7JLwAeOI"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Spread via Transistor Radio","body":"The emergence of the transistor radio in 1954 coincided precisely with the rise of Rock'n'Roll music. This portability allowed teenagers, whose parents often disapproved of the genre, to listen to the music away from home and parental supervision. The transistor radio became a crucial \"delivery vehicle\" for the widespread adoption and social impact of Rock'n'Roll, contributing to a \"social, demographic, economic, political, and sexual revolution.\" `\"The transistor radio appeared at exactly the same time as this new musical trend called Rock'n'Roll. Parents tended to hate this music. But thanks to the transistor radio, this new construct known as the teenager could listen to their music away from disapproving parents.\"`","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The History of Portable Music: Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OuFvfZJKcpc"}]}]}